{
  // ============================================================
  //  bclone — simulation config
  //
  //  Data-driven from day one (DESIGN.md §3): no tunable lives in
  //  code. Comments and trailing commas are permitted here —
  //  System.Text.Json is configured to allow both, so this file
  //  stays readable for modders without adding a dependency.
  // ============================================================

  // Seed for the whole run. Same seed + same config + same tick
  // count => byte-identical history. This is the determinism
  // contract (specs/tick-loop.md §1).
  "seed": 12345,

  // ---- The village ------------------------------------------
  // Four founding adults in two households, growing outward.
  "starting_households": 2,
  "adults_per_household": 2,
  "founder_age": 20,
  // ⭐ 15 -> 12 (Joe, D156). An uneducated child can work at twelve, which mirrors real life
  // and is the honest state of a game with no schooling in it. **When education lands, the
  // trade is that a schooled child starts LATER and is worth more when they do** — so this is
  // the dial that pair of choices will pull against, not a number that stays put.
  //
  // ⚠️ It buys hands and costs food: below this a villager eats `dependant_food_share_percent` of
  // a meal, so a twelve-year-old now eats a full one. Pairing and childbearing are NOT affected
  // — `leave_home_age` (18) and `fertility_min_age` (18) gate those separately.
  "adult_age": 12,
  "leave_home_age": 18,
  // ⭐ 7 -> 5 (Joe, D153). The house is what limits how many children a couple can have, and
  // at seven it almost never bound: measured, it refused only 1-3% of household-years while
  // the granary gate refused 42-70%. Five makes it a real lever and is the size the future
  // "bigger homes" unlock pushes on.
  //
  // ⭐ AND IT FIXES THE SHIPPED VILLAGE DYING OUT, for free. Over 300 unattended years this
  // config peaked at 27 and ended at ZERO, every death of old age; at five it ends at 20 with
  // nobody starving and nobody freezing. Measured across caps 3-7: three is below replacement
  // (extinct by year 30), and six or seven overrun the food.
  "max_household_size": 5,

  // Homes cluster in a spiral rather than a line, and the economy is
  // derived for the FURTHEST home a village this size will build - the
  // first version budgeted for household #1 and let every outlying
  // family starve.
  "economy_horizon_households": 20,

  // ---- Calendar ----------------------------------------------
  // 4 ticks/day x 30 days x 4 seasons = 480 ticks per year.
  //
  // THIRTY, doubled from fifteen (Joe, 2026-07-31), and it is a design
  // change rather than a tuning one. D45 states the danger of cold in
  // WEEKS - about two outdoors, about six sheltered without a fire - and
  // a fifteen-day season made that unsayable: six weeks was longer than
  // winter existed, so the model could not be expressed in the calendar
  // the game kept. A thirty-day season gives winter room for a threshold
  // to sit inside it.
  //
  // It re-derives the economy, in the order the derivation runs (food
  // before fuel): gather_yield 47 -> 46, stockpile_target 46 -> 95,
  // firewood_per_split unchanged at 22. See those keys for why each moved
  // as little or as much as it did.
  "ticks_per_day": 4,
  "days_per_season": 30,

  // ---- Playback only — NEVER read by sim logic ---------------
  // These control how fast the world is drawn, not what happens
  // in it. Changing them must not change sim outcomes; there is a
  // test that proves it (PlaybackSpeed_DoesNotAffectState).
  // Set so that AN AVERAGE LIFE TAKES 60 REAL MINUTES AT 4x (Joe,
  // 2026-07-31). That is the number that matters now, and it is a better
  // one to state than the old "a year takes 60 seconds": the generational
  // loop is the core loop (DESIGN.md §1.5), so how long a LIFE takes to
  // watch is the pacing decision and a year is just its unit.
  //
  // Derived, not picked: lifespan_years_base is 45, so a year must take
  // 3600/45 = 80 real seconds at 4x. 480 ticks/year / (1.5 * 4) = 80s.
  // With the +/-5 year spread, a life runs 53-67 minutes.
  //
  // Was 1.0 at a 240-tick year, which gave 60s/year and a 40-50 minute
  // life. Both numbers moved: the calendar doubled the ticks in a year,
  // and Joe asked for the longer life.
  //
  // What changes on screen is that a DAY passes faster - a year has
  // twice as many days in it and only a third more real time. That is
  // the honest consequence of a longer calendar, and it is one number
  // to put back.
  //
  // ---- SLOWED TO 0.75 (D74, Joe: "slow the whole game") -------
  // Joe, watching the cold start: "year 1 goes by way too quickly, it's
  // like 20 seconds at 10x." It was, and D49 above was working exactly
  // as specified - but D49 stated pacing for the STEADY STATE, where
  // the argument is that a life is the unit worth watching. The
  // founding is the one stretch of the game that asks the player to
  // act inside a single year, and 80 seconds is not long enough to
  // paint land, read the map, mark three buildings and work out why
  // nothing is happening.
  //
  // Halved, so in D49's own terms: a year is 160s at 4x and 64s at
  // 10x, and an average life takes about TWO HOURS at 4x rather than
  // one. That is the number to argue with if this feels wrong - the
  // life is the statement, the tick rate is only its unit.
  //
  // PLAYBACK ONLY. FixedTimestepDriver owns the single clock read and
  // no sim logic may look at this, so nothing derived moves and no
  // test changes: same seed, same run, same state hash, watched at a
  // different speed.
  "target_ticks_per_second": 0.75,
  "max_ticks_per_frame": 250,

  // ---- Needs -------------------------------------------------
  // The villager eats at 80 hunger, so at 7 a tick that is a meal
  // every 11 ticks - roughly every 2.8 days. That cadence is
  // chosen to be READ rather than calculated: you should be able
  // to watch it.
  //
  // SEVEN, NOT TEN. At ten it was a meal every two days, which read
  // as people permanently interrupting themselves to eat and left a
  // working day that did not look like work. Joe, watching a run:
  // "the hunger is too aggressive and maybe it should be slower."
  //
  // SEVEN IS ALSO WHERE IT STOPS BEING FREE, and that was measured
  // over 300 years rather than guessed. The village holds a band of
  // 17-32 at seven against 16-31 at ten - unchanged. Below that it
  // comes apart: 12-31 at six, 9-31 at five. Nobody starves at any
  // of them, so it is not a food failure. A slower-eating village
  // needs a smaller winter store per head, a smaller store is a
  // thinner buffer, and a thinner buffer swings harder. Slowing
  // hunger further means widening the population wave, and that is
  // a trade to make deliberately if it is made at all.
  //
  // Not a cosmetic dial: this sets what an adult eats in a year,
  // which is the number the whole food economy is derived from
  // (D16). gather_yield and stockpile_target below moved with it.
  "hunger_per_tick": 7,
  "hunger_max": 100,
  "eat_threshold": 80,
  "eat_reduces_hunger": 80,
  // ⭐ 5 -> 4 (Joe, 2026-08-23): "lower food consumption -20%".
  //
  // EXACTLY -20%, and the derivation makes it a REAL loosening rather
  // than a rename. RequiredGatherYield falls with it, and gather_yield
  // is guarded as a FLOOR rather than an equality -- so leaving the
  // yields where they are hands the village the whole 20% as headroom
  // above the survival line. Re-deriving the yields down too would have
  // been the same game with smaller numbers.
  "food_per_meal": 4,

  // 24 ticks = 6 days at maximum hunger before death. Boundary
  // is >= (decided in spec §11).
  "starvation_ticks": 24,

  // ---- Foraging ----------------------------------------------
  // DERIVED, not chosen. VillageEconomy states the target - one adult at
  // minimum vigour, with no partner, must feed themselves and three
  // children - and computes the yield it requires. A test asserts this
  // file still meets it, so editing hunger or travel without redoing
  // this fails the build rather than the village.
  //
  // The budget is the WORST walk in a village this size: for every home
  // it will plausibly build, how far is that home's own nearest site,
  // and which home has it worst. Both halves are load-bearing - deriving
  // from the first home starved the outskirts, and deriving from the
  // last home (which happens to sit beside a thicket) assumed everybody
  // had a one-tile commute and starved the whole village.
  // DERIVED, and re-derived every time an input moves (D16). 60 for the
  // hand-placed valley; 86 once the map was generated and the walk got
  // longer; 47 now that hunger runs slower and an adult eats 105 a year
  // rather than 150. Leaving it at 86 would have PASSED the test - the
  // guard only asks that the shipped value is enough - and quietly made
  // food a non-constraint, which is the opposite of what this game is
  // about.
  // 47 -> 46 when the season doubled, and the SIZE of that move is the
  // interesting part: a longer year means an adult eats twice as much
  // (105 -> 215) but also gets twice as many trips to gather it in
  // (9 -> 19), so the two nearly cancel. A trip is worth what a trip is
  // worth; lengthening the year does not change the physics of walking
  // to a berry patch. Set to the DERIVED value, not merely to one that
  // passes - the guard only asks for "enough", and leaving it at 47
  // would have made food fractionally free for no stated reason.
  //
  // 46 -> 51: the economy's bound stopped being an artefact of where a
  // generator dropped a berry patch and became the gatherer hut's own
  // ring, which the player can SEE (forests-and-gathering.md 3.2). Seven
  // tiles to eight, so a round trip is 19 ticks instead of 17 and 17
  // trips fit in a year instead of 19. Two fewer trips, each worth more.
  //
  // 51 -> 145 (Joe, 2026-08-11), and this one is not an adjustment.
  // The economy was derived for a FULLY WOODED ring, and no real hut is
  // ever fully wooded: measured, a well-sited warm-start hut yields 29 of
  // a possible 51 at founding and 19 by year twenty, because the village
  // clears its own ring as it builds. The village starved at year thirty
  // with 2,627 of the valley's 2,662 trees still standing.
  //
  // It is derived against forest_coverage_percent now - "one gatherer at
  // a hut whose ring is as wooded as the valley is" - which is true of an
  // averagely-sited hut and generous to a well-sited one. Siting a hut in
  // thick wood should be rewarded with slack rather than required to
  // break even.
  //
  // NOTE WHAT DID NOT CHANGE: a bald ring still yields nothing. This is
  // what the village budgets for, not what a trip is worth.
  "gather_yield": 145,
  // THE COPSE THINS UNDER USE (D257, Joe: "the copse of wood isnt infinite").
  // Every N completed gathers sets the nearest mature tree in the hut's ring
  // back to a sapling; regrowth grows it up again on its own sweep. So a hut
  // worked by seven foragers thins seven times as fast as one worked by one,
  // and the yield per trip falls with it -- gather_yield x wooded / ring.
  //
  // ZERO IS OFF, AND OFF IS WHAT SHIPS TODAY. The rate cannot be measured in an
  // unattended village: population there is capped by the GRANARY, not by food
  // (StorageTests.CapacityIsWhatHoldsThePopulationFlat), so no food lever moves
  // it until it is harsh enough to kill a poor valley. Joe plays the other
  // regime -- "as long as i keep adding granaries" -- which is where this bites.
  //
  // MEASURED at 3, averaged across a whole regrowth period so the sweep's phase
  // is out of it: a worked ring holds 49.8% mature wood against 55.8% unworked.
  // Lower is harsher. 1 makes the ring swing hard; 12 is barely visible.
  // Turning it on moves five goldens, so change it deliberately.
  "gathers_per_thinned_tile": 0,

  "gather_ticks": 3,
  "travel_ticks_per_unit": 1,

  // Also DERIVED: enough to carry one member through a foraging-less
  // winter, plus the margin that absorbs a worker dying part-way
  // through one. Per member, not per household.
  // Also derived, and it moved with hunger: a quarter of what an adult
  // eats in a year, plus the winter margin. 67 -> 46 -> 95.
  //
  // 46 -> 95 is the one number the longer season really does double, and
  // the reason is the one place where a longer year is NOT self-cancelling:
  // winter is twice as long, and nothing can be gathered during any of it.
  // Trips do not help you inside a season with no food in it. So the
  // store has to carry a household twice as far, and the granary that
  // holds it doubles with it (capacity 1410 -> 2850).
  "stockpile_target": 95,

  // ---- The valley --------------------------------------------
  // Wide rather than square: the terrain DESIGN.md §2.5 describes is
  // a river valley, and the river runs along it rather than across.
  //
  // Nothing in the sim reads these yet - they bound the camera and give
  // the drawn ground an edge, so a mostly-empty map reads as a valley
  // with room to grow rather than as a bug. They live here because the
  // MAP GENERATOR will need them (D18): terrain, water, forest stands
  // and forage sites all get generated into these bounds from the run's
  // seed, at which point this stops being a drawing hint and becomes
  // world state.
  "map_width": 120,
  "map_height": 80,

  // Where the village is founded, as an OFFSET from the spot the map
  // generator chose for it (D18). It used to be an absolute coordinate;
  // the valley is generated now, so the settlement is wherever the
  // generator put it and every building hangs off this.
  "home_x": 0,
  "home_y": 0,

  // ---- The valley, generated (D18) ---------------------------
  // These replaced the literal coordinates the world used to be typed
  // in as. A modder controls the RULES of a valley - how many sites,
  // how far out, how wide the river - rather than the outcome, which
  // is the honest data-driven form once the world is generated.
  //
  // RETIRED: forage_site_count, forage_site_ring_tiles, tree_stand_count,
  // tree_stand_ring_tiles, tree_stand_radius_tiles.
  //
  // Six berry patches and two stands of trees used to be drawn on a ring
  // here, and they were the last placeholder in the economy: food was a
  // FACT OF THE MAP rather than a decision. The valley is wooded across
  // its whole area now, the player sites a gatherer's hut in it, and the
  // trees inside that hut's ring decide what a trip is worth - so timber
  // and food compete for the same trees
  // (specs/forests-and-gathering.md, Joe: "no forest, no food").
  //
  // The old argument for several spread sites was about CATCHMENT - a
  // binding radius starves outlying homes if there is one food source.
  // Catchment is deleted too, and the argument went with it.
  //
  // See forest_coverage_percent and gatherer_hut_ring_tiles below: those
  // two are what the food economy is made of now.

  // EVERY TILE OF THIS IS PAID FOR BY THE WHOLE ECONOMY. The food
  // budget assumes the worst case - a site jittered directly away from
  // the home that needs it, costing twice this in Manhattan distance -
  // so generosity here makes every villager in every valley work
  // harder, including on the seeds where the sites land close. One tile
  // buys visibly different valleys; two pushed the required gather
  // yield up by two thirds.
  "site_jitter_tiles": 1,
  "founding_jitter_tiles": 2,

  // WATER IS IMPASSABLE (D40, D41), so a river is load-bearing rather
  // than scenery. Every travel query routes round it - labour catchment,
  // market errands, household placement and the economy's distance
  // budget all share one cost field, so they inherited it together.
  // Widening this genuinely lengthens the village's walks. Zero
  // generates no river at all, which is a supported valley.
  //
  // Crossing water will need a bridge, which is a technology first and
  // a building second, and waits on the tech tree.
  "river_width_tiles": 2,

  // Reserved for soil depletion (DESIGN.md §2.3). Generated and hashed,
  // read by nothing - it is here so that when depletion lands it does
  // not have to change the generator's DRAW ORDER, which would
  // invalidate every seed anyone had written down.
  "soil_quality_min": 40,
  "soil_quality_max": 200,

  // RETIRED: forage_site_capacity and forager_catchment_tiles.
  //
  // The first was how many hands fit at one berry patch, and there are
  // no patches. The second was how far it was reasonable to walk to
  // work, enforced as a refusal - and it is a COST now rather than a
  // fence: the allocator still claims the nearest hands first, but a
  // ruinous commute is a mistake the player can make, see and pay for
  // (D58, D120). Measured on the way out: the ten-tile fence was never
  // binding anyway - the furthest commute anybody held was three.

  // The village shares out all its work again every three years, so
  // people drift toward the jobs nearest where they live (decision D20).
  // Seasonal churn would make the stated reason for holding a job go
  // stale before anyone read it.
  //
  // THREE, up from one (D46, Joe). D20 already argued that a seasonal
  // reshuffle churns jobs faster than a player can read the reason for
  // holding one; a yearly one is the same objection at lower volume.
  // Three years is a rhythm a person can notice.
  //
  // Affordable because the two urgent cases are handled elsewhere and
  // do not wait for it: TakeUpSlack still runs at every season boundary,
  // so nobody sits idle beside an opening and the winter quota is acted
  // on the day the berry patches close (D44); and a job left vacant by a
  // death is filled the tick after it happens (D47).
  "labour_reshuffle_years": 3,

  // How often the village fills its openings and lets go of anybody it
  // no longer wants, IN TICKS. Sixty is fifteen in-game days.
  //
  // Joe, playing: "30 days feels unresponsive." It was hard-wired to a
  // season, so a change on the professions panel waited up to thirty days
  // to bite -- measured at 25, 15 and 5 days depending on where in the
  // season it was set.
  //
  // MEASURED ACROSS THREE SEEDS AT FIFTY YEARS, and the trade is churn for
  // responsiveness with nothing economic either way: population, food and
  // the share of able adults holding no job are all flat at 30, 10 and 5
  // days. What moves is job changes -- about 800 over fifty years at a
  // season, 1,220 at ten days, 1,960 at five.
  //
  // FIFTEEN, NOT TEN, AND THE BOUND IS MEASURED. At ten days D28's fix
  // stops working: two adults of one household share a tile 87% of ticks
  // with the seeded rhythm and mixed founding ON, against 87% with them
  // OFF -- the whole gain gone. At fifteen it is 90% -> 80%, exactly as
  // at a season. The hunger half (100% -> 0%) holds at every cadence.
  // D28 took four weeks to discharge and Joe confirmed it in play; it is
  // not worth trading for five more days of responsiveness.
  //
  // IT IS NOT THE RESHUFFLE. This pass never moves anybody who already
  // holds a job; it fills openings from the idle and sheds a surplus the
  // player asked to shed. D20 and D46 both argue against churning careers,
  // and neither is what this does.
  "labour_slack_ticks": 60,

  // What a learner gains per tick for working beside a MASTER of the
  // same trade at the same workplace. A hundred means they learn twice
  // as fast -- the sentence a player can hold in their head.
  //
  // This is DESIGN.md 2.1's whole point arriving: "that skill dies with
  // the person unless an elder apprentices a youth." Until it existed,
  // skill was personal and nothing transferred.
  //
  // MEASURED OVER A CENTURY ON THREE SEEDS. Masters alive at the end:
  // 3 -> 6, 4 -> 8, 8 -> 10 against a village with this at zero, and
  // 43-67% of learner-ticks are already spent beside a master, so the
  // mechanism has surface without anybody being assigned to anybody.
  // Population and food are flat.
  //
  // TWO HUNDRED IS TOO FAR: on seed 42 it ends the century with zero
  // food. A hundred leaves that seed at 1,485 against 1,513 with the
  // feature off.
  //
  // The master pays nothing (D183's "give, never take"), which is why
  // there is no policy dial -- a control with nothing to trade off is a
  // switch. The player's lever is staffing, and the at-risk line is what
  // tells them to use it.
  "apprentice_learning_bonus_percent": 100,

  // ---- Ageing ------------------------------------------------
  // Full strength until 30, then a straight decline to 55% in the
  // final year. It is what gives a life a shape: easy middle years
  // with time to rest, then a long tightening where the same food
  // costs more and more walking.
  "vigour_full_until_age": 30,
  "vigour_min_percent": 55,

  // ---- Life --------------------------------------------------
  // 67 +/- 12 => 55-79 year lives (Joe, 2026-08-11, watching Hattie die
  // at 42: "42 feels too young to die. let's extend it to 55-80").
  //
  // 55-79 rather than 55-80 because the draw is base +/- variance, so the
  // range is always an ODD number of years wide and 55-80 is 26. Erring
  // inside what was asked for rather than over it.
  //
  // Was 45 +/- 5 => 40-50, which D49 set when the pacing target was
  // stated as "an average life takes 60 real minutes at 4x". THAT TARGET
  // MOVES WITH THIS: a life is half as long again, so at the current tick
  // rate an average one runs about three hours at 4x rather than two.
  // The life is the statement and the tick rate is only its unit
  // (target_ticks_per_second is the number to argue with, not this one).
  //
  // It is not only pacing. Vigour declines from vigour_full_until_age to
  // vigour_min_percent at death, so a longer life is MORE YEARS SPENT
  // DECLINING - a village of the same size carries more low-vigour
  // adults. The economy is re-derived against it below.
  "lifespan_years_base": 67,
  "lifespan_years_variance": 12,

  // ---- Timber (decision D17) ---------------------------------
  // Cuttable year-round, unlike berries, so a woodcutter still works
  // in winter. Nobody is spared for the stand while a household in the
  // village is going hungry - the village feeds itself before it
  // builds, and that is the whole labour policy in one sentence.
  //
  // There are no stands any more: a forester works the ground their own
  // hut was given, and cut_yield is what one tree of it is worth.
  "cut_yield": 12,
  "rest_ticks": 3,

  // How long somebody with nothing to do sits before asking again. Raise it to make
  // the village visibly less busy; check it first if painted ground stops being cleared.
  "cut_ticks": 4,
  // DERIVED (VillageEconomy.RequiredForesterSeats), not picked. 3 -> 6.
  // Enough hands to keep the woodcutters in logs and still leave one for
  // building - the "+1" in the derivation is what stops the village
  // heating itself and never growing.
  //
  // It sizes the FORESTER'S HUT, and was called tree_stand_capacity until
  // D159 - the same question wearing the name of a mechanic that step C
  // deleted. Renamed for what it does.
  "forester_hut_capacity": 2,

  // A home has to be built, out of LOGS. Wood is two resources now
  // (D29): a logger fells logs, a woodcutter turns logs into firewood,
  // and firewood is what a household burns in winter. Logs build; only
  // firewood heats. Drawn from the whole village, parents first.
  "logs_per_house": 30,

  // ---- Woodland (forests-and-gathering.md) -------------------
  // The valley is WOODED, not dotted with two stands. Joe: "there
  // should be generated forests on the map naturally, just like stone,
  // iron, water -- lots of them, actually", so a gatherer's hut can be
  // sited in woodland from the first year.
  //
  // The coverage is the TARGET and is content; how many clumps that
  // takes is derived from it and the map's area
  // (MapGenerator.ForestClumpCount). Typing the count instead would
  // mean a bigger map quietly got a barer valley.
  //
  // It is a target rather than a promise: clumps are dropped
  // independently and overlap, and none may fall on water or on a
  // stone or iron seam, so what the valley actually ends up with is
  // lower than this -- and is asserted by a measurement rather than by
  // the arithmetic.
  "forest_coverage_percent": 35,

  // How big one wood is. Content, like tree_stand_radius_tiles: small
  // clumps give a mottled valley, large ones a few great forests.
  "forest_clump_radius_tiles": 4,

  // How much open ground the founding site keeps around it. MEASURED,
  // not chosen: with the valley wooded, 40 of the 81 tiles within four
  // of the founding site were forest, so every building the opening
  // marks waited on a clearing first -- the pile stood at t67 instead
  // of t1, the woodcutter's hut never stood at all, and ALL FOUR
  // FOUNDERS FROZE, against 4 alive and 2 roofed on bare ground. That
  // is D93's rule (any inserted hop kills winter 1) arriving from
  // worldgen rather than from labour.
  //
  // Exiles arriving in a river valley settle a glade. The woods begin a
  // few tiles out -- close enough for a gatherer's hut, far enough that
  // the opening is not a clearing puzzle.
  "founding_clearing_radius_tiles": 4,

  // ---- The gatherer's hut (forests-and-gathering.md) ---------
  // Where food comes from, and the first building whose yield depends
  // on the ground around it. The trees inside this ring decide what a
  // trip is worth -- half the trees, half the food -- and nothing
  // outside it counts for anything. No floor: a bald ring feeds nobody,
  // which is "no forest, no food" and the rule Joe asked for by name.
  // The safety is that it is VISIBLE on the hut's panel, not that it is
  // softened.
  //
  // The ring is content and it is the most load-bearing content in the
  // game: from slice 3 it also becomes the distance the whole food
  // economy is derived against, replacing the forage-site ring. A
  // number the player can see beats one that is an artefact of where a
  // generator dropped a berry patch.
  //
  // EIGHT, and the eight is chosen to keep that slice small:
  // MaxHomeToWorkTiles is 7 today, so moving the anchor to 8 changes
  // the round trip by one tile rather than re-deriving from nothing.
  //
  // How many hands fit is NOT here -- it is the ring priced in workers
  // (VillageEconomy.GathererHutCapacity), reusing D86's
  // work_ground_tiles_per_worker rather than inventing a second number
  // that could drift from it.
  "gatherer_hut_ring_tiles": 8,

  // What it costs. A real price, unlike the pile and the builder's hut
  // -- neither of those can be charged, because nothing can be built
  // without them, and a gatherer's hut has no such circle to break.
  "gatherer_hut_logs": 25,
  "gatherer_hut_work_ticks": 40,

  // ---- The forester's hut (forests-and-gathering.md) ---------
  // A wood somebody KEEPS, rather than one they only ever take from.
  // It fells the ground the player paints for it -- so timber has a
  // location and a quantity, and the gap you made is on the map -- and
  // with the mode switched it PUTS TREES BACK, which is the only thing
  // in this game that makes the valley richer than it found it.
  //
  // That is what makes over-clearing recoverable rather than terminal,
  // which is why it ships before the thickets retire: after that, a
  // felled ring is a village with no food.
  "forester_hut_logs": 25,
  "forester_hut_work_ticks": 40,

  // How much harder putting a tree back is than taking one down.
  //
  // CONTENT, and it is the number that decides whether over-clearing is
  // a mistake or a shrug -- nothing in the economy can compute what a
  // sapling is worth. What IS derived from it is the consequence:
  // VillageEconomy.YearsToRewoodOnesGround, which a guard holds to
  // about a generation.
  //
  // Three, so felling is a decision you can regret for years without
  // regretting the run. One would make planting free and a cleared
  // valley meaningless; ten would make it theoretical.
  "planting_costs_this_much_more_than_felling": 3,

  // ---- The farm (crops-and-orchards.md, D161) ----------------
  // A farm is a forester's hut with a different verb: a workplace
  // whose extent is painted work ground, sown in spring and reaped
  // in autumn. The year becomes something you watch happen instead
  // of a number that changes.
  //
  // What it costs. Priced like its siblings and deliberately not
  // free -- the pile and the builder's hut are free because nothing
  // can be built without them, and a farm has no such circle.
  "farmhouse_logs": 25,
  "farmhouse_work_ticks": 40,

  // How long a tile takes. CONTENT, in the same class as cut_ticks
  // and gather_ticks: how long an action takes is a fact about the
  // work. What IS derived from the pair is the consequence -- how big
  // a field one pair of hands can keep
  // (VillageEconomy.FieldTilesOneFarmerKeeps), which is what a farm's
  // work-ground allowance is priced against.
  //
  // BOTH THE SAME AS gather_ticks, deliberately: it is the same kind
  // of thing, one person doing one job on one tile, and pricing it
  // differently would be a claim about farming nobody could defend.
  // WHAT MAKES FARMING EXPENSIVE IS THE WALKING, and that is charged
  // where it happens -- a reaped tile is an armful carried back to
  // the steading, and one tile of crop is already more than
  // carry_capacity.
  //
  // The first draft made reaping dearer "because it is the half with
  // the load in it" and billed the load twice; the seam golden caught
  // it, measuring 84 tiles reaped in twenty years against 22 sown
  // every spring.
  "sow_ticks": 3,
  "reap_ticks": 3,

  // Food one tile of ripe field gives up.
  //
  // DERIVED, not chosen -- VillageEconomy.RequiredCropYield says
  // what it must be, and ShippedConfigTests checks this file against
  // it. The target is a comparison and that is what makes it
  // derivable at all: A FARMER'S YEAR IS WORTH A GATHERER'S YEAR.
  //
  // IT IS A BIG NUMBER BECAUSE A REAPED TILE IS A WALK. farm_store_cap
  // is 100 and a tile yields more than half of it, so the buffer takes
  // the first armful of an autumn and every reap after it is a trip to
  // the granary -- measured, a farmer brings in about five tiles a
  // season whatever the size of the field. Four tiles times two seats
  // is an eight-tile field, and that field feeds about two households.
  //
  // The lever if this reads wrong is farm_store_cap or letting a
  // farmer fill their arms before walking, not this number.
  "crop_yield_per_tile": 67,

  // How much of its own harvest a farm holds before the walk gets
  // longer (Joe: 100).
  //
  // It is the first Workplace.Store in the project's history that
  // anything writes to -- professions.md 4's fifth element, on the
  // type since D30 and dead ever since. A buffer, not a
  // destination: the granary is still where food lives, and the 100
  // means something because the farmer hauls to the nearest storage
  // WITH ROOM, so the store underfoot fills first.
  // How many people fit in a farmhouse. CONTENT, not a
  // derivation, and it used to be the other way round: deriving it
  // from max_household_size / MouthsFedByOneAdult gave 1, which is
  // arithmetically fine and a bad building — a workplace with one
  // seat reads as broken rather than as small. How many pairs of
  // hands fit in a steading is a fact about the world; what they
  // produce is what gets derived.
  "farmhouse_seats": 2,

  "farm_store_cap": 100,

  // ---- The builder's hut (D108) ------------------------------
  // Where the village's builders work from. Nothing is raised without
  // one: a construction site is an errand the hut's crew walks out to,
  // not a place anybody is assigned to, so a village with no hut can
  // mark out as much as it likes and watch none of it rise.
  //
  // Only a WARM start reads these. The game as it ships starts the
  // founders in an empty valley, and placing the hut is the player's
  // first act -- it costs nothing but the ground it stands on, because
  // it is the building every other building waits on.
  //
  // How many hands fit in it is NOT here: it is derived from the economy
  // (VillageEconomy.BuilderHutCapacity) -- the hands left over once the
  // village has fed and heated itself, which is what building has always
  // been funded from. A position is content; a capacity is a
  // consequence, and woodcutter_hut_capacity below is the recorded case
  // of what typing one costs.
  "builder_hut_x": -1,
  "builder_hut_y": -1,

  // ---- Firewood (decision D29) -------------------------------
  // The first workplace that CONSUMES an input rather than only
  // producing one. It can stand idle for want of logs, which is a state
  // no other workplace can be in - so a woodcutter with an empty yard
  // says so rather than mysteriously going home.
  //
  // Near the homes rather than near the stand: logs are drawn
  // village-wide, so the only distance that costs anything is the
  // woodcutter's daily walk from home.
  "woodcutter_hut_x": -2,
  "woodcutter_hut_y": 1,
  // DERIVED (VillageEconomy.RequiredWoodcutterSeats), not picked. 3 -> 8.
  //
  // THE YIELDS WERE RE-DERIVED AND THE CAPACITIES WERE NOT, and that gap
  // had been open since the economy horizon was raised to twenty
  // households. Three seats heats about fifteen households; the economy
  // is budgeted for twenty. A village that reached that size could not
  // physically make more firewood however many hands were free - the
  // failure VillageEconomy.RequiredWoodcutterSeats already documents,
  // where thirty-six people froze.
  //
  // It was LATENT rather than harmless: the village only holds around
  // thirty people, so it never reached the size that would bite. What
  // changed is that the player can now BUILD GRANARIES (D33, D43) and
  // grow past the old ceiling on purpose, which walks them straight into
  // it. A pressure with no answer available is one thing; a pressure the
  // player unlocks by succeeding is a trap.
  //
  // Asserted against the shipped file now, not just the test fixture -
  // the two diverge, and this gap lived entirely in that divergence.
  "woodcutter_hut_capacity": 2,
  "logs_per_split": 6,
  "split_ticks": 4,

  // DERIVED, like gather_yield. The stated target: heating every home
  // through winter must cost no more than HALF the hands the village can
  // spare once everyone is fed. The half is the margin, and it is the
  // same argument D16 makes about the third dependant - solving for
  // "exactly affordable" produces a village that is warm by construction
  // with nobody left to build and no slack for a long winter, which is
  // useless the moment anything pushes on it.
  // Re-derived twice: 36 -> 141 when the valley became generated and the
  // haul to the shed got longer, then 141 -> 22 when hunger slowed. Fewer
  // meals means fewer ticks lost to eating, so a woodcutter fits far more
  // batches into a year and each one can be much smaller.
  //
  // 22 -> 25 with the bound re-based on the gatherer's ring
  // (forests-and-gathering.md 3.2).
  //
  // ORDER MATTERS HERE AND IT CAUGHT ME OUT. Read against the OLD
  // gather_yield this key demanded 41; read again after food was
  // re-derived to 51 it wanted only 25. Fuel is budgeted out of the hands
  // the village has SPARE, and spare hands is a difference - hands, minus
  // the hands it takes to feed everyone. Fix food first and the
  // subtraction leaves more behind, so fuel gets cheaper. Setting 41
  // would have over-configured firewood by 64% and called it derived.
  //
  // The header of this file already said "in the order the derivation
  // runs (food before fuel)". It says it because of exactly this.
  "firewood_per_split": 50,

  // Per household, not per member: a house costs the same to heat
  // whether two live in it or five. That makes SPRAWL the thing that
  // costs rather than population - a pressure that traces back to a
  // player decision (DESIGN.md §2.3).
  //
  // Still one log per burn, and still the switch that turns fuel off
  // entirely when it is zero (Phase 0's world has no winter fuel).
  "firewood_per_winter_day": 1,

  // How many days one burn lasts (Joe, 2026-08-11: "make firewood
  // consumption take longer. like 4x longer. it goes too fast").
  //
  // The rate had to move HERE rather than in the amount above, because
  // sim state is integer-only (D2) and a quarter of a log is not a
  // number this game can hold. Four days per log is the same four-times
  // slower burn expressed in a unit the sim has.
  //
  // The economy follows it: FirewoodPerHouseholdPerWinter divides by it,
  // so a winter costs a household a quarter of what it did, and every
  // number derived from that - the store target, the woodcutter seats,
  // the foresters feeding them - comes down with it.
  //
  // ⭐ 4 -> 3 (Joe, 2026-08-23): "increase firewood consumption +20%".
  //
  // ⚠️ IT IS +25%, NOT +20%, AND THE GRID IS WHY. A winter costs
  // ceil(days_per_season / interval) x firewood_per_winter_day, so with
  // a 30-day winter the only reachable numbers at one log a burn are
  // 8 (interval 4), 10 (interval 3) and 15 (interval 2). Ten is the
  // closest to +20% and it is what shipped.
  //
  // Exactly +20% is reachable only as 3 logs every 10 days, which hits
  // the number and changes the RHYTHM: a household would need three in
  // hand at once instead of one, so a thin larder fails in bursts
  // rather than gradually. That is a different feel, not a finer dial,
  // so the closer-in-spirit option won. Say so if the burst is wanted.
  "firewood_burn_interval_days": 3,

  // ---- Regrowth (D125) ---------------------------------------
  // The valley comes back. Measured before this existed: a village
  // felled its own gatherer's ring simply by living in it - houses,
  // sites and the forester's ground are all near the hut - and
  // thirteen people starved beside a valley still 97% wooded.
  //
  // Sixty days is half a year, and one sweep is both stages of a
  // tree: grass touching wood becomes a sapling, and a sapling that
  // has stood a whole sweep becomes wood. So a cleared tile is a
  // sapling within six months and a mature tree within a year, which
  // is the rate Joe asked for ("it should come back every year").
  //
  // Zero switches it off.
  // ⭐ 60 -> 72 (Joe, 2026-08-25): "trees grow back a little too fast. slow them
  // down by 20%% or so."
  //
  // This is ONE RUNG of the ladder, not the whole climb, which is why 20%% here is
  // 20%% of a year rather than of six months: a cleared tile becomes a SAPLING after
  // one period and mature FOREST after a second (D125), and a sapling yields nothing
  // to anybody. So the wait for harvestable wood goes from 120 days to 144 -- a full
  // year to 1.2 years, on a 120-day calendar.
  "regrowth_period_days": 72,

  // ---- Cold is a place you are standing (D45) -----------------
  // freezing_ticks is GONE. It was one counter per household - "days
  // since this family ran out of firewood" - asked identically of
  // everyone, everywhere, so a villager froze because of a number
  // attached to their relatives rather than because of anything you
  // could watch happen. These two replace it, and they are stated in
  // DAYS rather than ticks because they describe a human being in the
  // cold, not a tick rate. The tick rate has already moved once (D49).
  //
  // Fifteen days outdoors is half a winter: going out is dangerous
  // without being immediately fatal, which is what makes clothing worth
  // wanting later (D19/D39) rather than mandatory now.
  //
  // Twenty-five sheltered is LESS THAN A THIRTY-DAY WINTER, and that is
  // the number's job: an unheated house can still kill you inside one
  // season. Above thirty and CauseOfDeath.Cold would go dormant until
  // clothing shipped, taking D17's whole reversal with it.
  //
  // Zero on either switches that half off - the market_capacity: 0
  // pattern - which is how the world clothing eventually creates gets
  // tested before the clothing chain exists.
  "exposure_days_outdoors": 15,
  "exposure_days_sheltered": 25,

  // Days at a burning hearth to come back FROM THE BRINK of freezing
  // (Joe, 2026-08-23: "fire warm up should be much faster than it
  // currently is").
  //
  // THE THIRD NUMBER IN D45's TABLE, AND THE ONE THAT WAS NEVER STATED.
  // Fifteen days outdoors kills, twenty-five under a fireless roof
  // kills, and five at a fire brings you all the way back. Three lines,
  // one model.
  //
  // It used to be DERIVED by mirroring the outdoor rate, on the stated
  // grounds that mirroring "needs no number of its own" -- which was
  // true, and quietly chose fifteen anyway. Half a winter to thaw. A
  // derivation that avoids stating a number still states one, and this
  // is what that costs when nobody checks what it came out as.
  //
  // Bounded below by a measurement rather than by taste: a fire that
  // zeroed the count outright was built and rejected, because villagers
  // spend 76% of winter at a lit hearth and nobody froze in 120 years.
  // Five days is fast; instant is a reset.
  "thaw_days_at_a_fire": 5,

  // How far toward freezing somebody gets before they drop what they are
  // doing and walk to the nearest fire. Halfway is where the old system
  // already printed its "you are cold" line, so the player has been
  // trained on it; now it means something rather than merely being said.
  //
  // Not a tuning knob for difficulty: it is the point at which a person
  // decides work can wait. Lower and nobody works through a winter at
  // all; higher and the walk to the fire is itself what kills them.
  "seek_shelter_percent": 50,

  // ---- Storage (D30, D32) ------------------------------------
  // Two buildings, not one. Food spoils and timber does not, and one
  // undifferentiated village pile would delete the per-household
  // inequality D14 exists to create - "one family starving beside a
  // thriving neighbour" is the story per-household food was introduced
  // for. Keeping them apart changes what inequality is MADE of: not
  // whose larder it is, but who is near the granary and who has hands
  // to send.
  //
  // These place the FOUNDING granary and shed, near the homes, so a
  // village always has somewhere to put things. Every store after them
  // the player sites themselves (D43) - and WHERE the granary goes is
  // the first decision in the game that storage makes interesting.
  "granary_x": 1,
  "granary_y": -1,
  "storage_shed_x": -2,
  "storage_shed_y": 0,

  // What one person can carry in one trip. This is the whole reason a
  // fetch is a journey rather than a teleport with extra steps: an
  // armful at a time is what makes the distance to the granary cost
  // something, which is where D32 said the interesting inequality would
  // live. Raise it far enough and storage becomes bookkeeping again.
  "carry_capacity": 40,

  // HOW BIG THE VILLAGE CAN GET, stated as a fact about a building (D33).
  //
  // A granary holds a winter's store for this many people; VillageEconomy
  // derives the quantity from it, and derives the population ceiling from
  // that. Births are gated on the granary holding a share of what everyone
  // alive would want, so once the village outgrows what the building can
  // hold, it stops growing. That is the whole mechanism, and it is what
  // makes the population hold a flat line instead of overshooting and
  // falling back: measured over 200 years, 24-35 people here against
  // 24-86 with the cap removed.
  //
  // A number about the BUILDING, like how many hands fit at a berry patch -
  // which is why it is here rather than derived. What is NOT typed in is the
  // consequence: the player answers a ceiling they have hit by building a
  // second granary (D33, D43), which prices the buffer rather than capping
  // it (D39). This line is the price per granary, not the village's limit.
  // ⭐⭐ A SIZE, NOT A PROMISE (D219, Joe): "it's fine if the granary feeds a
  // different number of people. The user should build more granaries -- and will
  // need to! -- and upgrade them."
  //
  // Was `granary_feeds_people: 30`, multiplied by a winter's ration to reach a
  // quantity. The trouble with that: a village that ate more got a BIGGER granary
  // for free, because the building resized itself to keep its promise. A granary is
  // a box. How many people it feeds is a consequence of how much they eat, so a
  // village that eats more now needs MORE GRANARIES -- which is D39's "the buffer is
  // priced, not capped" applied to the building, and what gives an upgrade tier
  // something to be.
  //
  // 2500 is a round number Joe chose, sitting between what the shipped config used
  // to derive (2310) and what the test fixture derived (2850) -- see D219 for why
  // those two ever differed, which is a food drift this change made visible.
  "granary_capacity": 2500,

  // ---- The market (D14, D36) ---------------------------------
  // Among the homes, which is the whole of its value: it is the
  // short walk. The one building that is both a store and a
  // workplace, because its contents arrive by somebody's work
  // rather than by producers dropping things off.
  "market_x": 2,
  "market_y": 1,

  // More than one trader - a market is a place several people work.
  // ZERO SWITCHES IT OFF, and that is a supported state with a test
  // on it: households still fetch for themselves, so an unstaffed
  // market means longer walks and stranded goods, never a family
  // that cannot eat. The moment that stops being true, the market
  // has become the cliff fetching was designed to avoid.
  "market_capacity": 2,

  // What the market keeps on hand, per household the village is
  // budgeted for. A market is a short trip, not a second granary -
  // sized flat it would become the real store as the village grew,
  // re-centralising everything storage just spread out.
  "market_stock_per_household": 40,

  // ---- What buildings cost to raise (D43) --------------------
  // Two numbers each, on purpose. Logs are what the village must
  // HAVE; work ticks are what it must SPEND. A building that is
  // dear in one and cheap in the other is a different decision
  // from one that is dear in both, and a single "cost" would
  // delete that difference.
  //
  // Nothing appears the instant it is paid for: the player marks a
  // site, materials are carried to it, and somebody builds it.
  // That is the same claim D14 makes about distribution and D29
  // about processing - things that happen here are work somebody
  // does. It also means building COMPETES with foraging, so
  // marking six buildings at once is a decision rather than six
  // purchases.
  "granary_logs": 40,
  "granary_work_ticks": 60,
  "shed_logs": 30,
  "shed_work_ticks": 45,
  "market_logs": 35,
  "market_work_ticks": 50,
  "hut_logs": 25,
  "hut_work_ticks": 40,

  // The stone half of a recipe (D213). ONLY THE STORES THE PLAYER MARKS: a
  // granary, a shed and a market are durable civic buildings and Joe's own
  // TECH-EXAMPLE prices even a root cellar in wood AND cut stone. The huts and
  // the house pay nothing, and that split was measured rather than chosen --
  // see the block above granary_stone in SimConfig.cs for the fifty-year
  // numbers. A village that never marks a store never needs stone; one that
  // marks a granary it cannot pay for keeps running and is told what it wants.
  "granary_stone": 10,
  "shed_stone": 8,
  "market_stone": 10,

  // And a nominal amount on the huts (Joe, D214). One seam tile is 12 stone,
  // so clearing a single rock buys four huts. Measured over fifty years: the
  // village holds 24 either way, and a founding that never paints a seam simply
  // builds FEWER huts -- a pressure you can read and recover from, not a death.
  "hut_stone": 3,
  "gatherer_hut_stone": 3,
  "forester_hut_stone": 3,
  "farmhouse_stone": 3,

  // construction_site_capacity is GONE (D108). A site had three seats and
  // people were assigned to it; builders hold their job at the hut now and
  // treat sites as errands, so the number would be permanently zero -- and
  // a number that is always zero is a lie waiting to be found (D98).

  // How much painted ground one worker can keep (D86). The first limit in
  // this game that is not distance -- everything else asks how far somebody
  // must walk; this asks how much they can look after. It is what makes the
  // staffing control matter: paint more ground, hire more hands.
  //
  // The ceiling it implies is derived rather than typed: a workplace cannot
  // be staffed past its capacity, so the most ground anybody can hold is
  // capacity times this, and "to a limit" needs no second number.
  "work_ground_tiles_per_worker": 24,

  // Logs from clearing one forest tile (D87). A forest tile is a deposit and
  // this is what is in it: take it and the ground is grass, where a tree
  // stand yields cut_yield forever. Matching cut_yield is the honest start --
  // one tile is one visit to the stand.
  //
  // Content today, DERIVED the moment the tree stand retires: per-tile yield
  // is what the whole timber economy gets re-derived against, and while
  // stands still stand the brush is an extra source rather than the only one.
  //
  // ⭐⭐ MOVED (D210): this is `yield_per_tile` on the LOGS row of the goods
  // catalogue now, along with stone's and iron's. See `goods-catalog.md`.
  //
  // ⚠️ The goods themselves are NOT listed in this file, deliberately -- their
  // defaults live in `SimConfig.GoodsCatalog` the way `skills`, `household_names`
  // and `town_names` do, so there is ONE source of truth rather than two that can
  // drift (METHODOLOGY §3, and D48/D49/D50 are what drift costs). To change them,
  // add a "goods" array here and it replaces the list wholesale.

  // Visible seams of stone and iron (D67, D84, D90). RULES, NOT
  // COORDINATES: a modder controls how much ore a valley has and roughly
  // where, never which tile.
  //
  // Seams rather than scatter, and never a percentage roll -- you can see
  // a seam, so going after it is a decision rather than a lottery.
  //
  // STONE NEAR, IRON FAR, and that is design rather than scarcity for its
  // own sake. Reaching the iron is something the player chooses to do, and
  // a valley whose ore sits in the far woods plays differently from one
  // where it is on the doorstep.
  //
  // Laid only over open grass -- never over forest, because taking trees
  // out of the valley would be a balance change hiding inside a worldgen
  // change, and the whole fuel economy is derived against reachable wood.
  // What one tile of each seam holds moved to the goods catalogue with logs
  // (D210) -- it is `yield_per_tile` on the STONE and IRON rows now. Less iron
  // per tile than stone, and fewer iron seams: together those are what make
  // iron worth walking for rather than merely further away, and the second
  // half of that pair is still right here.

  "stone_seam_count": 4,
  "stone_seam_ring_tiles": 14,
  "stone_seam_radius_tiles": 2,

  "iron_seam_count": 2,
  "iron_seam_ring_tiles": 26,
  "iron_seam_radius_tiles": 1,

  // The land the exiles arrive having already chosen to live on (D42).
  // A village founded with nothing painted could never build a house,
  // and the first thing the game asked of a player would be a decision
  // they had no basis for. It is also a gentle tutorial: you see what a
  // zone looks like before being asked to paint one.
  //
  // FOUR IS A MEASURED FLOOR. At three, ten of eleven seeds held and
  // the eleventh died out - and the way it died matters: a village that
  // cannot spread cannot form new households, so every home fills to
  // max_household_size, births stop, and the settlement ages out. At
  // four, a village nobody helps behaves as it always has, and the
  // brush becomes necessary when the player builds more granaries and
  // grows past it.
  "starting_residential_radius": 4,

  // THE COLD START (D70). The founders arrive to an empty valley with a cart:
  // no granary, no shed, no market, no woodcutter's hut, no homes, and nothing
  // painted to build them on. Every one of those is the player's to raise, and
  // starting_residential_radius above is not applied at all while this is false.
  //
  // Fixtures leave this true, so the several hundred tests written about an
  // established village go on testing one (Joe's call). That is exactly the
  // fixture-versus-shipped gap METHODOLOGY 3 warns about, so ColdStartTests
  // exists to cover this path and asserts that THIS file says false.
  "founding_buildings": false,

  // What the founders arrive carrying, and how much the cart holds. These are
  // the difficulty dial for the opening, together with the founding season --
  // the exposure rates are NOT a dial and must not be reached for instead, for
  // the reason D53 records: they describe a person in the cold, and the death
  // rate is a property of the economy.
  //
  // The capacity is small on purpose. The cart accepts every kind of goods,
  // unlike every other store, so its size rather than its rules is the only
  // thing stopping it being the granary and the shed at once.
  //
  // The tools are NOT a dial and cannot be one yet: nothing in the game makes
  // or spends them, so they are a stock the player can see and cannot use. The
  // founders brought them; a workshop to replace them is D17's, still parked.
  //
  // `cart_logs` is gone rather than sitting at 0 (D90 step 4): the cart REFUSES
  // timber now, so a key holding a number the wagon would not accept is a lie
  // waiting to be found. It is a food-and-tools box, which is what you arrived
  // in.
  "cart_capacity": 1650,
  "cart_food": 1200,
  "cart_tools": 20,

  // NO cart_stone (Joe, D215): "there should already be stone on the map for the
  // user to ask the laborers to harvest. I don't want stone in the cart. Only
  // food and tools." There is -- stone_seam_count 4 on a fourteen-tile ring.

  // Less than everything, deliberately. Demolition is how a player
  // corrects a mistake, and it should cost something - otherwise a
  // badly sited granary is free to undo and the placement decision
